# COMPONENT v2 — one wing authored once, placed twice (v1.22).
#
# The whole building is three statements of composition. `museum-wing.arch` draws ONE
# gallery wing in its own local coordinates; this file imports that FILE as a component
# and places it on both sides of a shared entrance hall — the east one MIRRORED, so the
# pair reads as one symmetrical building instead of two copies facing the same way.
#
# Read this the way you would read a React page: `wing` is the component, `place … as`
# is the element, `at`/`mirror` are its props, and `west.corridor` is how the parent
# reaches a child by name. Nothing about the wing changed to make it reusable.
#
#   west wing  x 0…18000     hall  x 18000…24000     east wing  x 24000…42000
#
# The east wing is `mirror x`, not `rotate 180`: mirroring flips it left↔right so its
# corridor still runs along the SOUTH edge facing the hall, while a 180° turn would swing
# the corridor round to the north and leave the galleries between it and the entrance.
# A mirror is a real reflection — the fire exit and every door swing come out
# mirror-image, which is exactly what a symmetrical building wants.
plan "Museum — two wings" {
  units mm
  grid 100
  paper A1 landscape
  north up
  dims auto all
  schedule rooms

  # A whole FILE as a component: no `component` declaration, no parameters, no wrapper.
  import "museum-wing.arch" as wing

  # Structural datums: the wing faces, the hall faces, and the corridor spine.
  axes {
    x at 0, 18000, 24000, 42000
    y at 0, 9000, 12000
  }

  title {
    project "Municipal museum — ground floor"
    drawn_by "ArchLang"
    date "2026-07-26"
  }

  # The two wings. Each `place` is one addressable instance: every id inside it becomes
  # `west.<id>` / `east.<id>`, with auto-id counters restarting per instance, so the two
  # are order-independent.
  #
  # Each instance is also implicitly a zone, which is how `describe --zone west` reaches
  # its rooms — but an instance is a NAMESPACE, not a heading, so the room schedule does
  # not group by one (six placed consult rooms would otherwise print six one-row groups
  # with six subtotals; `examples/clinic.arch` records that case). Grouping is something
  # a drawing says out loud, so the `zone` around each `place` is what puts "West wing"
  # and "East wing" on the schedule. It costs nothing else: a zone has zero geometric
  # semantics, and the instance ids are unchanged — `west.shell` is still `west.shell`,
  # because id namespacing follows the INSTANCE path, not the zone path.
  zone west "West wing" {
    place wing() as west at (0,0)
  }
  zone east "East wing" {
    place wing() as east at (42000,0) mirror x
  }

  # The entrance hall between them. It borrows the wings' own shells for its east and west
  # sides — the only walls it declares are the two it does not share.
  wall id=hall_north exterior thickness 300 { (18000,0) (24000,0) }
  wall id=hall_south exterior thickness 300 { (18000,12000) (24000,12000) }

  room id=hall at (18000,0) size 6000x12000 label "Entrance hall" uses entry hall

  # Main entrance, and the two doors that join the hall to each wing's corridor. These are
  # the composition: the parent cuts them, by DOTTED reference into the instances' shells
  # (`west.shell` is the wing's `wall id=shell`, namespaced by `as west`).
  # `swing into hall` rather than `swing in`: `in`/`out` are relative to the host wall's
  # TRAVERSAL direction, so the correct answer here depends on which way `hall_south` was
  # written. Naming the room says what is meant and cannot be broken by editing the wall.
  #
  # The main entrance is a DOUBLE door: two 1000 mm leaves meeting at a shared closed jamb
  # on the axis (x=21000), each hinged on its outer jamb, so they are mirror images.
  # `hall_south` runs west→east, so the west leaf's outer jamb is `hinge left` and the east
  # leaf's is `hinge right`. The two swings touch only at the shared jamb, which is not an
  # obstruction. (`grid 100` rounds a door's `at` to the hundred, so the leaves are an even
  # 1000 mm: 900 mm leaves would land 50 mm off the axis.)
  door id=d_main_w at (20500,12000) width 1000 wall hall_south hinge left  swing into hall
  door id=d_main_e at (21500,12000) width 1000 wall hall_south hinge right swing into hall
  door id=d_west at (18000,10500) width 1800 wall west.shell  hinge left  swing into hall
  #
  # `d_east` is the mirror image of `d_west`. `east.shell` runs the opposite way to
  # `west.shell` (the wing is `mirror x`), so the SAME word `hinge left` hangs the leaf on
  # the mirrored jamb. With the main pair mirrored too, the whole building — shell, rooms
  # and every door — is D1 x about the hall's axis (`arch describe --facts symmetry`).
  door id=d_east at (24000,10500) width 1800 wall east.shell  hinge left  swing into hall

  window id=w_hall_n at (21000,0) width 3000 wall hall_north
}
